  <title>5.12.6: Lesson Summary</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01470</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.12.6: Lesson Summary</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9756b1fa-9059-411d-8ee9-9026430be8d3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you learned how to:</para>
<list>
  <item> Describe the effect of changing \(a\) and \(b\) on a graph that represents \(f(x)=a \cdot b^x\). </item>
  <item> Use equations and graphs to compare exponential functions. </item>
</list>
<para>Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that helped you reach those goals:</para>
<list>
  <item> 5.12.1: Modeling Exponential Decay </item>
  <list>
    <item> In this activity, you practiced modeling a situation characterized by exponential decay with an equation. </item>
  </list>
  <item> 5.12.2: Equations and Their Graphs </item>
  <list>
    <item> In this activity, you examined how the numbers \(a\) and \(b\) influence the graph representing a function \(f\) defined by an equation of the form \(f(x)=a \cdot b^x\). </item>
    <item>5.12.2: Self Check</item>
    <item> 5.12.2: Additional Resources  </item>
  </list>
  <item> 5.12.3: Graphs Representing Exponential Decay </item>
  <list>
    <item> In this activity, you explored how the parameter \(b\) influences the graph of an exponential function, which prepared you to make sense of and model situations involving repeated percentage. </item>
    <item>5.12.3: Self Check</item>
    <item> 5.12.3: Additional Resources  </item>
  </list>
  <item> 5.12.4: Possible Equation for a Function on a Graph </item>
  <list>
    <item> In this activity, you determined the graphed equation that defined a given function. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>After these activities, you completed the following <emphasis>practice</emphasis>:</para>
<list>
  <item> 5.12.5: Practice </item>
</list>
<title>Checking In</title>
